  "configuration_id": "config_031",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best (config_019, acc=0.639) uses pca_n2000 as the RNA encoder, and all completed multimodal experiments share this encoder. The dominant design decisions (multiprofile, avgpool aggregation, weighted fusion) are well-established, but the RNA encoder is the largest untested variable. Question: Does replacing PCA with scvi_n200 (a learned, biologically-informed latent representation) improve performance when all other settings match the current best? Why this experiment: config_031 (scvi_n200, resnet50, avgpool, weighted, multiprofile) is a clean single-variable ablation against config_019, isolating the RNA encoder effect. This is the highest-information next step because the RNA encoder has the largest remaining uncertainty—if scvi_n200 helps, it opens a productive exploitation path (scgpt, etc.); if it does not, PCA is confirmed as sufficient and we can redirect budget to other dimensions. It avoids the smaller, already-nearly-resolved image-encoder question (resnet50 vs ViT-L differ by only ~0.4%)."
}

